// PREDICATED SORT — RESULT positions, func AND const: `func op2(a: H, b: H): H`
// asserts op2 is closed on H (like an uninterpreted func's signature IS an axiom).
// At each use `op2(x,y)`, `inH(op2(x,y))` is surfaced as an available fact, so a
// downstream `f(op2(x,y))` (f needs an H arg) composes — the subgroup-closure
// pattern, for free. Sound: the arg-obligations GATE it (op2's args must be in H),
// and it is exactly equivalent to op on G + an explicit closure axiom cited by
// hand. Kernel-checked, no accelerated taint.
sort G
pred inH(g: G)
sort H = G where inH

func op2(a: H, b: H) => H
func f(z: H) => G

axiom useful: forall h: H; f(op2(h, h)) = f(op2(h, h))

// THE decisive corner case: f(op2(h,h)) type-checks — op2(h,h) is in H (surfaced),
// so f accepts it; op2's own args discharge from the binder guard inH(h).
theorem closureComposes: forall h: H; f(op2(h, h)) = f(op2(h, h))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall h: H; f(op2(h, h)) = f(op2(h, h))
    [by cite useful]
qed

// A predicated-result CONST is the nullary case of the SAME mechanism: `const E: H`
// asserts E ∈ H, surfacing inH(E), so f(E) composes.
const E: H
axiom usefulE: f(E) = f(E)
theorem constComposes: f(E) = f(E)
proof
  @conclusion |
    f(E) = f(E)
    [by cite usefulE]
qed
